WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.175
Lesson 2.4.

00:00:02.175 --> 00:00:05.475
Impermanent loss and true LP profit and loss.

00:00:05.475 --> 00:00:08.502
By the end, you'll calculate an LP position's full

00:00:08.502 --> 00:00:11.530
result, and judge it fairly against simply holding.

00:00:12.830 --> 00:00:14.120
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.455
What impermanent loss actually measures,

00:00:16.455 --> 00:00:18.235
and the formula behind it.

00:00:18.235 --> 00:00:21.302
The full L.P. profit and loss formula, every

00:00:21.302 --> 00:00:24.670
real component, not just the headline number.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.548
A complete worked example, with real numbers,

00:00:27.548 --> 00:00:29.850
from entry to a real price move.

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:32.610
And the one honest question that separates a

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:34.880
good LP decision from a lucky market.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:37.745
What IL actually measures.

00:00:39.045 --> 00:00:42.630
Here's the precise definition, worth getting exactly right,

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.380
since the name itself is easy to misread.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.013
Impermanent loss is how much your L.P.

00:00:48.013 --> 00:00:51.185
position lags simply holding the same starting

00:00:51.185 --> 00:00:54.590
tokens, once their relative price has changed.

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.465
It isn't measured against zero.

00:00:56.465 --> 00:00:59.191
It's measured against the specific alternative

00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:01.105
of never having deposited at all.

00:01:02.405 --> 00:01:06.359
Here's exactly why the name includes “impermanent.” The gap

00:01:06.359 --> 00:01:10.248
between your L.P. position and simply holding only becomes

00:01:10.248 --> 00:01:13.645
a real, locked-in loss the moment you withdraw.

00:01:13.645 --> 00:01:15.607
If the price ratio moves back to

00:01:15.607 --> 00:01:18.164
where it started before you withdraw, that

00:01:18.164 --> 00:01:20.605
gap closes completely, back to zero.

00:01:20.605 --> 00:01:23.631
Withdraw while the ratio is still different from your

00:01:23.631 --> 00:01:27.050
entry, and what was impermanent becomes permanent, instantly.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.638
Here's the formula itself, broken into

00:01:30.638 --> 00:01:32.933
the two numbers it actually needs.

00:01:32.933 --> 00:01:34.285
Take r, the new price

00:01:34.285 --> 00:01:36.305
divided by your entry price.

00:01:36.305 --> 00:01:39.884
Impermanent loss equals two times the square root of

00:01:39.884 --> 00:01:42.615
r, divided by one plus r, minus one.

00:01:42.615 --> 00:01:44.732
Feed in any price ratio, and this

00:01:44.732 --> 00:01:47.472
formula alone tells you the percentage lag,

00:01:47.472 --> 00:01:50.337
before fees, before gas, before anything else.

00:01:51.637 --> 00:01:55.492
Worth knowing this formula doesn't care which direction price

00:01:55.492 --> 00:01:58.465
moves, only how far the ratio has diverged.

00:01:58.465 --> 00:02:01.409
E.T.H. rising from three thousand to four

00:02:01.409 --> 00:02:04.002
thousand, a ratio of one point three

00:02:04.002 --> 00:02:07.105
three, gives about negative one percent.

00:02:07.105 --> 00:02:09.939
E.T.H. falling from three thousand to two

00:02:09.939 --> 00:02:13.246
thousand two hundred fifty, the exact reciprocal

00:02:13.246 --> 00:02:16.515
ratio, gives that same negative one percent.

00:02:16.515 --> 00:02:19.830
Divergence causes it, in either direction, symmetrically.

00:02:21.130 --> 00:02:23.400
Plot the formula across a wider range

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:25.550
of price moves, and the shape makes

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.060
the “either direction” point obvious.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:31.505
Barely any divergence, barely any impermanent loss.

00:02:31.505 --> 00:02:34.425
As the ratio moves further from one, in either

00:02:34.425 --> 00:02:37.594
direction, the loss grows, and it grows faster the

00:02:37.594 --> 00:02:41.135
further you go, the same bending-curve shape from Lesson

00:02:41.135 --> 00:02:44.304
two point two, just applied to your total position

00:02:44.304 --> 00:02:45.795
now, not a single trade.

00:02:47.094 --> 00:02:48.184
Quick check.

00:02:48.184 --> 00:02:50.434
E.T.H. doubles in price. What's

00:02:50.434 --> 00:02:52.017
the impermanent loss on a

00:02:52.017 --> 00:02:54.149
fifty-fifty pool, from the formula?

00:02:58.569 --> 00:03:01.104
The answer: about negative five point seven

00:03:01.104 --> 00:03:03.709
percent, versus simply holding. r equals

00:03:03.709 --> 00:03:06.012
two, and the formula gives two

00:03:06.012 --> 00:03:08.314
root two over three, minus one.

00:03:09.614 --> 00:03:11.019
Full LP P&L.

00:03:12.319 --> 00:03:15.270
Here's the full formula, every component that

00:03:15.270 --> 00:03:18.714
actually determines whether L.P.ing was worth it.

00:03:18.714 --> 00:03:22.015
Start with your L.P. position's current value, which

00:03:22.015 --> 00:03:24.994
already has impermanent loss baked into it.

00:03:24.994 --> 00:03:27.219
Add every fee you've earned along the way.

00:03:27.219 --> 00:03:30.904
Add any extra incentive rewards the protocol paid you.

00:03:30.904 --> 00:03:34.234
Subtract gas, for depositing, and for withdrawing.

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:37.184
And that total is what you actually compare against

00:03:37.184 --> 00:03:39.964
simply holding, not any single piece of it alone.

00:03:41.264 --> 00:03:42.918
Worth naming the trap this

00:03:42.918 --> 00:03:44.994
full formula exists to close.

00:03:44.994 --> 00:03:48.261
Looking only at your L.P. position's dollar value

00:03:48.261 --> 00:03:51.659
today, up twenty percent since you deposited, tells

00:03:51.659 --> 00:03:53.919
you almost nothing on its own.

00:03:53.919 --> 00:03:56.799
That twenty percent could be entirely the

00:03:56.799 --> 00:04:00.090
underlying tokens rising in price, with L.P.ing

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:03.312
itself having actively cost you, once compared

00:04:03.312 --> 00:04:06.740
honestly against simply holding those same tokens.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.540
Here's how to actually track this weekly,

00:04:10.540 --> 00:04:12.745
in practice, not just in theory.

00:04:12.745 --> 00:04:16.458
Record your L.P. position's current value, and the price

00:04:16.458 --> 00:04:19.755
ratio it implies, every week, on the same day.

00:04:19.755 --> 00:04:22.774
Record fees and any incentive rewards earned since

00:04:22.774 --> 00:04:26.270
the last check-in, separately from the position value.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.009
And calculate what simply holding your original

00:04:29.009 --> 00:04:31.120
deposit would be worth today, as the

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.801
one number everything else gets judged against.

00:04:35.101 --> 00:04:37.738
One more honest factor the full formula

00:04:37.738 --> 00:04:41.100
surfaces: gas matters far more, proportionally, on

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.641
a small position than a large one.

00:04:43.641 --> 00:04:45.929
Twenty dollars of combined gas on a

00:04:45.929 --> 00:04:49.044
six-thousand-dollar position is about a third of

00:04:49.044 --> 00:04:51.441
a percent, a real but small drag.

00:04:51.441 --> 00:04:55.041
That same twenty dollars on a six-hundred-dollar position

00:04:55.041 --> 00:04:57.585
is over three percent, enough on its own

00:04:57.585 --> 00:04:59.819
to wipe out a modest fee APR before

00:04:59.819 --> 00:05:01.681
anything else is even counted.

00:05:02.981 --> 00:05:04.071
Quick check.

00:05:04.071 --> 00:05:05.453
Your LP position is up

00:05:05.453 --> 00:05:07.856
twenty percent since you deposited.

00:05:07.856 --> 00:05:09.301
Did LPing itself work?

00:05:13.721 --> 00:05:16.271
The answer: unknown, until you compare it

00:05:16.271 --> 00:05:18.757
with simply holding the same tokens.

00:05:18.757 --> 00:05:21.258
That twenty percent could be entirely from

00:05:21.258 --> 00:05:23.177
the price rising, not from LPing.

00:05:24.477 --> 00:05:25.482
Worked example.

00:05:26.782 --> 00:05:28.750
Here's the starting position this

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:30.787
entire worked example runs on.

00:05:30.787 --> 00:05:35.173
1 ETH, plus 3,000 USDC, deposited

00:05:35.173 --> 00:05:38.827
at an ETH price of $3,000.

00:05:38.827 --> 00:05:43.257
Total starting value: $6,000, split evenly between the two.

00:05:44.557 --> 00:05:48.406
Now E.T.H. rises to four thousand dollars, and follow

00:05:48.406 --> 00:05:51.842
exactly what the pool does to your position.

00:05:51.842 --> 00:05:55.073
It rebalances you to zero point eight six six

00:05:55.073 --> 00:05:59.568
E.T.H., and three thousand four hundred sixty-four U.S.D.C., by

00:05:59.568 --> 00:06:04.082
the same constant-product formula from Lesson two point two.

00:06:04.082 --> 00:06:07.403
Your L.P. position's value: zero point eight six six

00:06:07.403 --> 00:06:11.538
times four thousand, plus three thousand four hundred sixty-four,

00:06:11.538 --> 00:06:15.047
comes to six thousand nine hundred twenty-eight dollars.

00:06:16.347 --> 00:06:18.361
Now add fees and gas, the parts

00:06:18.361 --> 00:06:21.746
a bare impermanent-loss number always leaves out.

00:06:21.746 --> 00:06:24.779
This position earned one hundred fifty dollars in

00:06:24.779 --> 00:06:27.449
fees along the way, and cost twenty dollars

00:06:27.449 --> 00:06:30.661
in gas, for depositing and withdrawing combined.

00:06:30.661 --> 00:06:32.921
LP total, all in: six thousand nine

00:06:32.921 --> 00:06:36.186
hundred twenty-eight, plus one hundred fifty, minus

00:06:36.186 --> 00:06:39.450
twenty, comes to seven thousand fifty-eight dollars.

00:06:40.750 --> 00:06:45.494
Now compare that $7,058 against two different baselines, since

00:06:45.494 --> 00:06:49.710
only one of them actually judges the LPing decision.

00:06:49.710 --> 00:06:54.454
Against your starting $6,000, you're up $1,058, which

00:06:54.454 --> 00:06:58.005
is mostly ETH simply rising in price.

00:06:58.005 --> 00:07:03.190
Against simply holding, $7,000, you're up only $58.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:05.900
That $58, not the $1,058, is

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.265
the actual value LPing itself added.

00:07:10.565 --> 00:07:13.662
So here's the one sentence a master actually judges

00:07:13.662 --> 00:07:16.880
this position on, and it isn't the bigger number.

00:07:16.880 --> 00:07:18.917
A master judges the L.P. on the

00:07:18.917 --> 00:07:22.655
fifty-eight dollars, not the one thousand fifty-eight.

00:07:22.655 --> 00:07:24.520
The market move isn't a skill.

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:27.217
Beating simply holding, even by a little, is

00:07:27.217 --> 00:07:29.555
the only part that was actually earned.

00:07:30.855 --> 00:07:33.367
Worth naming what could have gone the other

00:07:33.367 --> 00:07:35.936
way in this exact same worked example, since

00:07:35.936 --> 00:07:39.090
a positive fifty-eight dollars was not guaranteed.

00:07:39.090 --> 00:07:42.381
If thirty-day volume had been lower, fees earned might

00:07:42.381 --> 00:07:45.673
have been thirty dollars instead of one hundred fifty,

00:07:45.673 --> 00:07:49.143
which would have left this position at negative sixty-two

00:07:49.143 --> 00:07:52.494
dollars against simply holding, a real loss from LPing,

00:07:52.494 --> 00:07:54.470
on the very same price move.

00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:56.686
The fee income is what has to outrun

00:07:56.686 --> 00:07:59.500
the impermanent loss, not the other way around.

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:01.890
Quick check.

00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:03.916
When does impermanent loss actually

00:08:03.916 --> 00:08:05.886
become a permanent, locked-in loss?

00:08:10.306 --> 00:08:13.232
The answer: the moment you withdraw while the price

00:08:13.232 --> 00:08:16.176
ratio is still different from your entry ratio.

00:08:16.176 --> 00:08:17.668
If it moves back to entry

00:08:17.668 --> 00:08:19.561
first, the gap closes completely.

00:08:20.861 --> 00:08:22.321
Here's your checklist.

00:08:22.321 --> 00:08:24.556
Do it now, and keep doing it weekly.

00:08:24.556 --> 00:08:27.345
Track your P&L against simply holding, every

00:08:27.345 --> 00:08:29.566
week, not just once at the end.

00:08:29.566 --> 00:08:32.887
Record impermanent loss, fees, incentives and gas

00:08:32.887 --> 00:08:35.711
separately, not as one blended number.

00:08:35.711 --> 00:08:38.829
And write your exit rule now: if fees consistently

00:08:38.829 --> 00:08:42.131
fail to cover impermanent loss, that's your signal to

00:08:42.131 --> 00:08:44.271
leave, not a reason to wait it out.

00:08:45.571 --> 00:08:48.355
One practical note before the recap, so you're

00:08:48.355 --> 00:08:51.321
not doing this arithmetic by hand every week.

00:08:51.321 --> 00:08:55.832
This program's own calculator tool runs both formulas directly:

00:08:55.832 --> 00:09:00.132
the impermanent-loss percentage for any price ratio, and the

00:09:00.132 --> 00:09:03.163
fee A.P.R. needed to break even by a given

00:09:03.163 --> 00:09:05.366
day, at a given ratio move.

00:09:05.366 --> 00:09:08.086
Feed it real numbers, instead of re-deriving

00:09:08.086 --> 00:09:10.141
the formula from memory each time.

00:09:11.441 --> 00:09:12.686
Let's recap.

00:09:12.686 --> 00:09:15.915
Impermanent loss measures how far your LP position

00:09:15.915 --> 00:09:18.890
lags simply holding, once price has moved, and

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:21.596
it's only permanent once you withdraw.

00:09:21.596 --> 00:09:24.459
Full LP P&L is LP value, plus fees,

00:09:24.459 --> 00:09:29.691
plus incentives, minus gas, compared honestly against holding.

00:09:29.691 --> 00:09:33.014
In the worked example, a thousand-dollar-plus gain

00:09:33.014 --> 00:09:36.141
was mostly the market; only fifty-eight dollars

00:09:36.141 --> 00:09:38.331
was actually earned by LPing.

00:09:38.331 --> 00:09:41.603
And that fifty-eight dollars, not the bigger number, is

00:09:41.603 --> 00:09:44.291
what a master actually judges the position on.

00:09:45.591 --> 00:09:48.816
Track your P&L against simply holding, every week,

00:09:48.816 --> 00:09:52.151
and judge yourself on what LPing actually added.

00:09:52.151 --> 00:09:56.466
Next up, Lesson 2.5: MEV and protecting your trades.
